meta {
  name: Send Password Reset
  type: http
  seq: 12
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/users/{{activeUserId}}/password-reset
  auth: bearer
  body: none
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
    // Dev/test only — absent in production, where the email is the only
    // delivery channel. See the docs block.
    bru.setVar("passwordResetToken", res.getBody().resetToken);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("the reset URL is absolute and points at the reset page", function () {
    // An email client has no origin to resolve a relative path against. The
    // invite flow shipped a relative URL once; this is the guard against
    // repeating it.
    expect(res.getBody().resetUrl).to.match(/^https?:\/\//);
    expect(res.getBody().resetUrl).to.contain("/auth/reset-password?token=");
  });

  test("expires in the future, and within about a day", function () {
    const ms = new Date(res.getBody().expiresAt).getTime() - Date.now();
    expect(ms).to.be.greaterThan(0);
    // Hours, not the invite's 7 days. A generous ceiling so a locally raised
    // PASSWORD_RESET_EXPIRY_HOURS does not fail the run, but tight enough to
    // catch someone wiring the invite's day-based expiry in by mistake.
    expect(ms).to.be.lessThan(48 * 60 * 60 * 1000);
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /users/:userId/password-reset

  Email an **active** employee a link to set a new password (PAC-79).

  **Module** — none (`@SkipModule`). **Permission** — `agency:users:write`
  (Agency Owner). A producer or CSR gets `403`.

  **Body** — none.

  **Response `201`**
  ```json
  {
    "userId": "...",
    "resetUrl": "http://localhost:5173/auth/reset-password?token=<hex>",
    "expiresAt": "2026-08-31T09:00:00.000Z",
    "resetToken": "<hex>"
  }
  ```

  > ⚠ `resetToken` is returned **only when `NODE_ENV` is not `production`**. It
  > is a bearer credential and the email is its delivery channel; it is exposed
  > outside production so the flow can be walked locally and in e2e. The same
  > predicate governs `inviteToken` on **Invite User**.

  ## Why this endpoint exists
  The SmartSuite migration wrote `randomBytes(24).toString('hex')` into
  `passwordHash` — not a bcrypt digest, so `bcrypt.compare` can never match.
  Those users cannot log in, and cannot be re-invited either, because the invite
  endpoints refuse an active user. This is the only way back in for them.

  ## What is stored, and what is not
  The database holds a **SHA-256 digest** of the token, never the token. That is
  a deliberate divergence from `inviteToken`, which is stored raw: a database
  read — a backup, a support query — must not yield a working credential.

  Issuing a reset also clears any pending invite token, keeping the invariant of
  one live credential per account.

  **Errors**
  - `403` — caller lacks `agency:users:write`.
  - `404` — no such user in this agency, a malformed id, or the target is a
    platform admin.
  - `409` — **two different cases, with different remedies:**
    - the user was *removed* from the agency ("Reactivate them first") — a
      removed employee must not be able to walk back in through this flow;
    - the user has not *accepted their invite* yet ("Resend the invite
      instead").
  - `409` — a reset was issued for the same user inside
    `PASSWORD_RESET_COOLDOWN_SECONDS` (default 60). The message names the wait.
    The per-IP throttle is not the meaningful limit here; the inbox being
    protected belongs to the employee, not the caller.

  ## Chaining
  `activeUserId` is the demo producer, captured by **List Users** (seq 9). By
  this point **Deactivate User** and **Reactivate User** have run, so they are
  active again. The token captured here is consumed by **Get Password Reset
  (Public)** and **Reset Password (Public)**.
}
